WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.030 --> 00:00:02.750
Film Courage: What is The Truth Behind The
Lie?

00:00:02.750 --> 00:00:07.389
Mark W. Travis, Director/Author/Writer/Instructor:
The Truth Behind The Lie…okay in autobiographical

00:00:07.389 --> 00:00:16.219
storytelling one thing we are very strict
about to a point is we are telling the true

00:00:16.219 --> 00:00:17.219
story.

00:00:17.219 --> 00:00:20.610
We are sticking to the truth of the characters,
sticking to the truth of characters, sticking

00:00:20.610 --> 00:00:21.990
to the truth of events.

00:00:21.990 --> 00:00:26.170
We are not making up stuff.

00:00:26.170 --> 00:00:37.710
But there are times when we actually fictionalize
the truth (fictionalize the truth - in other

00:00:37.710 --> 00:00:42.399
words lie) to get closer to a deeper truth.

00:00:42.399 --> 00:00:46.129
In other words, I’ll give you an example.

00:00:46.129 --> 00:00:57.800
When I was developing A Bronx Tale about young
Calogero and his father and the gangster (Sonny).

00:00:57.800 --> 00:01:05.880
And I said to Chazz [Chazz Palminteri] I need
a story about your father.

00:01:05.880 --> 00:01:12.570
We need some kind of story or event with your
father that actually shows how he is dealing

00:01:12.570 --> 00:01:21.180
with the conflict that you have with this
gangster and how he is dealing with the gangster.

00:01:21.180 --> 00:01:24.280
And he said “Well there isn’t any story.

00:01:24.280 --> 00:01:26.409
There isn’t any story like that."

00:01:26.409 --> 00:01:33.850
There isn’t any event (he said) where they
got together or they confronted the boy.

00:01:33.850 --> 00:01:36.420
I said “Well then we have to create one.”

00:01:36.420 --> 00:01:43.340
And what we did is we created a story so we
could get closer to the truth of how the father

00:01:43.340 --> 00:01:44.860
felt about this.

00:01:44.860 --> 00:01:51.729
Chazz knew how he felt about this but by creating
a story that really didn’t happen, in a

00:01:51.729 --> 00:01:58.189
scene it didn’t happen and in the film these
scenes are still there that didn’t actually

00:01:58.189 --> 00:02:01.520
happen because they are there to serve a purpose.

00:02:01.520 --> 00:02:07.329
The purpose of these fictional scenes or these
fictional exchanges and sometimes it even

00:02:07.329 --> 00:02:12.670
comes down to we will put words in a character’s
mouth that they never said but those words

00:02:12.670 --> 00:02:15.120
reveal the truth of the character.

00:02:15.120 --> 00:02:21.340
That is when we will fictionalize to get closer
to the truth and that’s allowed.

00:02:21.340 --> 00:02:25.010
Film Courage: How do writers feel about this?

00:02:25.010 --> 00:02:31.099
Mark: Well you are talking about the autobiographical
part?

00:02:31.099 --> 00:02:36.049
First of all the writer is the character who
lived through this.

00:02:36.049 --> 00:02:40.920
Usually they feel fine about it because many
times I will say okay in this situation if

00:02:40.920 --> 00:02:46.819
your father said this…let’s say it’s
a little boy (this is not from A Bronx Tale),

00:02:46.819 --> 00:02:49.331
a father and a mother.

00:02:49.331 --> 00:02:56.040
Let’s say an argument going on between the
little boy and what happens if the mother

00:02:56.040 --> 00:03:00.200
was in that scene or heard that scene, what
would she have said?

00:03:00.200 --> 00:03:02.660
It would have been awful.

00:03:02.660 --> 00:03:04.010
Really?

00:03:04.010 --> 00:03:06.650
What would she have said and he would tell
me.

00:03:06.650 --> 00:03:10.230
And he would say give me an idea of what she
would have said?

00:03:10.230 --> 00:03:11.560
She would have been horrified and furious.

00:03:11.560 --> 00:03:16.030
He said fine, let’s put her in the scene.

00:03:16.030 --> 00:03:18.590
But she wasn’t in the scene.

00:03:18.590 --> 00:03:26.780
But we are trying to get closer to what the
dynamics are and if we put her in the scene

00:03:26.780 --> 00:03:33.280
we will try and experiment and many times
the writer (getting back to your question)

00:03:33.280 --> 00:03:34.860
will go Wow!

00:03:34.860 --> 00:03:36.959
That’s good, that’s good.

00:03:36.959 --> 00:03:38.480
That’s what would have happened.

00:03:38.480 --> 00:03:39.489
That’s who they are.

00:03:39.489 --> 00:03:44.500
And if I can get to that point where the writer
says that is really who they are, that is

00:03:44.500 --> 00:03:51.470
the truth of who they are then I feel fine
and they feel fine because they have honored

00:03:51.470 --> 00:03:55.040
the truth of the characters, maybe not the
truth of the events.

00:03:55.040 --> 00:03:57.549
Film Courage: What is a gap?

00:03:57.549 --> 00:03:59.120
Mark: The gap!

00:03:59.120 --> 00:04:01.099
Film Courage: The gap?

00:04:01.099 --> 00:04:04.390
Not the store and not a ‘gap year.’

00:04:04.390 --> 00:04:06.270
Mark: I like the t-shirt…not the t-shirt.

00:04:06.270 --> 00:04:08.360
I used to have a t-shirt.

00:04:08.360 --> 00:04:12.920
In London at the tube “mind the gap.”

00:04:12.920 --> 00:04:19.560
When I started to realize something very important,
this was long ago when I was doing these autobiographical

00:04:19.560 --> 00:04:20.560
pieces.

00:04:20.560 --> 00:04:22.280
I was working on one or two of them.

00:04:22.280 --> 00:04:23.650
I forgot which one.

00:04:23.650 --> 00:04:25.199
It doesn’t matter.

00:04:25.199 --> 00:04:30.180
When I realized that I knew what the events
were, I knew what happened.

00:04:30.180 --> 00:04:33.289
They told me this is what happened.

00:04:33.289 --> 00:04:38.090
But then I realized that something was missing
in the telling of the story.

00:04:38.090 --> 00:04:46.560
What was missing was from the protagonist
(the main character, the autobiographical

00:04:46.560 --> 00:04:47.560
character).

00:04:47.560 --> 00:04:54.620
I wasn’t hearing what they really wanted
to have happen or hoping would happen or expecting

00:04:54.620 --> 00:04:56.110
to happen.

00:04:56.110 --> 00:05:01.100
In other words if it’s a confrontation.

00:05:01.100 --> 00:05:07.650
Let’s make it very simple, it’s a guy
telling a story and it’s a story about asking

00:05:07.650 --> 00:05:13.910
a beautiful young woman to the prom with him.

00:05:13.910 --> 00:05:16.250
He tells me what happens and what she said.

00:05:16.250 --> 00:05:22.110
I realize what is missing in that story was
what he thought was going to happen.

00:05:22.110 --> 00:05:23.220
What he hoped would happen.

00:05:23.220 --> 00:05:26.180
What he fantasized would happen.

00:05:26.180 --> 00:05:35.680
Now in other words his pursuit is to ask her
to the prom and he wants her to say yes.

00:05:35.680 --> 00:05:41.350
Prior to telling the whole story we need to
know what he thinks is going to happen.

00:05:41.350 --> 00:05:48.840
How he is going to pursue that so we are writing
with that character inside his belief, inside

00:05:48.840 --> 00:05:53.550
his expectation, inside his desire so we are
writing with him.

00:05:53.550 --> 00:05:58.289
If he leaves this out and he just says I am
going to ask her out then we don’t know

00:05:58.289 --> 00:06:02.319
how important this is, we don’t know what
is riding on it.

00:06:02.319 --> 00:06:05.120
We don’t know what he is expecting if he
says yes.

00:06:05.120 --> 00:06:07.770
We don’t know what he is expecting if she
says no.

00:06:07.770 --> 00:06:13.990
If we don’t know that, half of the scene
is gone, half of the energy is gone.

00:06:13.990 --> 00:06:20.889
What will happen…what I realize when I started
exploring this is this is the we we ALL go

00:06:20.889 --> 00:06:22.970
through life.

00:06:22.970 --> 00:06:26.090
We all go through life no different than this
interview.

00:06:26.090 --> 00:06:30.099
All of us in this room (the four of us in
this room) had an expectation of how this

00:06:30.099 --> 00:06:31.660
interview would turn out.

00:06:31.660 --> 00:06:35.990
And I can tell you right now we were all wrong.

00:06:35.990 --> 00:06:37.910
It has turned out differently.

00:06:37.910 --> 00:06:42.740
That’s all, just different.

00:06:42.740 --> 00:06:45.560
Just different.

00:06:45.560 --> 00:06:50.990
If we look at life as we move through life,
this is life, we have a pursuit we are going

00:06:50.990 --> 00:06:51.990
to do.

00:06:51.990 --> 00:06:54.650
We talk about going to lunch, we will have
this meal.

00:06:54.650 --> 00:06:58.690
Immediately we have ideas about what it is
going to be like.

00:06:58.690 --> 00:07:02.270
Then I have to deal with life as it happens.

00:07:02.270 --> 00:07:05.300
It is going to be different.

00:07:05.300 --> 00:07:08.290
The thing you order will not be on the menu.

00:07:08.290 --> 00:07:11.030
The restaurant will not look like you wanted
it.

00:07:11.030 --> 00:07:12.770
You will have to serve yourself.

00:07:12.770 --> 00:07:15.039
There is no waiter…whatever.

00:07:15.039 --> 00:07:16.150
Things are different.

00:07:16.150 --> 00:07:25.950
So the gap, to put it in the simplest of terms
is expectation versus result.

00:07:25.950 --> 00:07:30.800
What does the character expect and then what
is the result?

00:07:30.800 --> 00:07:32.560
And how is that different?

00:07:32.560 --> 00:07:39.830
The truth is we all live with the gap every
single day.

00:07:39.830 --> 00:07:45.970
Every single day as part of our daily experience
this is what is going to happen.

00:07:45.970 --> 00:07:50.479
All of us in this room now because we are
in the middle of the afternoon have an expectation

00:07:50.479 --> 00:07:52.889
of what is going to happen for the rest of
the day.

00:07:52.889 --> 00:07:58.039
I can tell you right now it won’t work out
the way you think it’s going to.

00:07:58.039 --> 00:07:59.470
Something different will happen.

00:07:59.470 --> 00:08:00.470
That’s life.

00:08:00.470 --> 00:08:07.650
So I realize in constructing these stories,
these autobiographical stories, I needed to

00:08:07.650 --> 00:08:13.879
expose to the audience through the storyteller
what the character wants and needs and why

00:08:13.879 --> 00:08:21.370
it is so important and what they are expecting,
very specifically with that naive narrator

00:08:21.370 --> 00:08:24.610
talking to the audience saying I am going
to go ask her for the date.

00:08:24.610 --> 00:08:27.560
It’s going to be great because she is not
going to expect that I’m going to ask her

00:08:27.560 --> 00:08:32.450
at all and she is going to be so thrilled
because I know she likes me.

00:08:32.450 --> 00:08:37.150
I am just giving you a lot of information
as to what this character is imagining and

00:08:37.150 --> 00:08:39.920
dreaming will happen.

00:08:39.920 --> 00:08:45.450
And as that story goes along it gets to the
point of asking her, he gets to the point

00:08:45.450 --> 00:08:49.890
of are you going to go to the prom?

00:08:49.890 --> 00:08:53.140
She says oh yes I am.

00:08:53.140 --> 00:08:54.440
Oh great.

00:08:54.440 --> 00:08:55.980
Do you want to go with me?

00:08:55.980 --> 00:09:01.780
Now we are at that point where he thinks that
she is going to be thrilled.

00:09:01.780 --> 00:09:03.790
And what does she say?

00:09:03.790 --> 00:09:06.350
Why would I do that?

00:09:06.350 --> 00:09:10.180
Now BIG gap, right there!

00:09:10.180 --> 00:09:16.570
If we don’t know how much he is invested
in this the response will mean less.

00:09:16.570 --> 00:09:24.440
We have to be carrying his investment into
the scene with him and so we are hit by those

00:09:24.440 --> 00:09:27.680
obstacles as he navigates through that scene.

00:09:27.680 --> 00:09:36.620
Film Courage: It reminds me…I had a college
professor that wrote on a chalkboard “Expectations

00:09:36.620 --> 00:09:37.620
= Disappointments.”

00:09:37.620 --> 00:09:40.920
Now this sounds a little negative but I think
in some ways.

00:09:40.920 --> 00:09:41.920
Mark: It’s true.

00:09:41.920 --> 00:09:44.360
Film Courage: Yes, if you approached it this
way, then maybe you wouldn’t get your hopes

00:09:44.360 --> 00:09:45.360
dashed?

00:09:45.360 --> 00:09:51.340
I put that saying up on a little paper on
my desk at an old job I used to have and someone

00:09:51.340 --> 00:09:53.500
said “Oh that’s so negative?”

00:09:53.500 --> 00:09:55.660
And I thought maybe it’s being more of realist?

00:09:55.660 --> 00:09:58.200
Mark: Flip it around.

00:09:58.200 --> 00:10:03.250
Imagine Karen that you have expectations for
something, something you want, something you

00:10:03.250 --> 00:10:10.060
are going to do, a pursuit, and maybe it’s
the simplest thing…I’ll make it very simple.

00:10:10.060 --> 00:10:13.190
You are going to cook a meal for you and David.

00:10:13.190 --> 00:10:15.520
It’s a meal you have never cooked before.

00:10:15.520 --> 00:10:17.360
Film Courage: Uh-oh.

00:10:17.360 --> 00:10:22.950
Mark: But you have the recipe, you have all
of the ingredients, your expectation is it’s

00:10:22.950 --> 00:10:23.950
going to be fantastic.

00:10:23.950 --> 00:10:25.600
It’s going to be gourmet.

00:10:25.600 --> 00:10:28.640
You make the whole thing and it’s all great
and it looks good and it smells good and you

00:10:28.640 --> 00:10:33.500
serve it and it is gourmet and it’s exactly
what you wanted.

00:10:33.500 --> 00:10:34.500
Is that interesting?

00:10:34.500 --> 00:10:36.430
Film Courage: No.

00:10:36.430 --> 00:10:42.020
Mark: The thing is if we always get the expectations
or the characters always get what they expect

00:10:42.020 --> 00:10:47.170
it’s not an interesting story because it
has no relationship to life.

00:10:47.170 --> 00:10:49.300
Film Courage: That’s true.

00:10:49.300 --> 00:10:51.970
It’s too perfect, too planned.

00:10:51.970 --> 00:10:54.110
It probably wouldn’t happen.

00:10:54.110 --> 00:10:58.600
Mark: But the thing is if you are fixing this
gourmet meal a and it looks good and it smells

00:10:58.600 --> 00:11:02.250
good and David’s coming home with take out
“Ah!

00:11:02.250 --> 00:11:04.980
I got dinner.”

00:11:04.980 --> 00:11:05.980
There is a gap.

00:11:05.980 --> 00:11:06.980
Film Courage: There is.

00:11:06.980 --> 00:11:09.280
Mark: Then you go “Oh no.”

00:11:09.280 --> 00:11:10.280
Disappointment.

00:11:10.280 --> 00:11:11.360
Film Courage: Right.

00:11:11.360 --> 00:11:16.800
Mark: So I think the disappoint thing and
that is what we live with every day.

00:11:16.800 --> 00:11:19.260
It’s not a negative, it’s a reality.

00:11:19.260 --> 00:11:21.560
Things do not turn out.

00:11:21.560 --> 00:11:25.390
And it doesn’t mean things are worse, it
just means they are different.

00:11:25.390 --> 00:11:26.390
It could be better?

00:11:26.390 --> 00:11:33.340
A gap is just if you were making that gourmet
meal and you’re going “Why am I doing

00:11:33.340 --> 00:11:34.340
this?”

00:11:34.340 --> 00:11:36.990
Now your expectation is it’s going to be
a disaster, you’ve never made it before

00:11:36.990 --> 00:11:41.930
and you don’t have quite the right ingredients
and you are using olives instead of grapes

00:11:41.930 --> 00:11:45.560
and it turns out wonderful, well there’s
a gap?

00:11:45.560 --> 00:11:48.060
Film Courage: It’s true.

00:11:48.060 --> 00:11:50.810
Expectations equal disappointments.

00:11:50.810 --> 00:11:52.520
Mark: So there is the gap.

00:11:52.520 --> 00:12:00.800
It’s very important in not just autobiographical
I think in any…to me as I look at a screenplay

00:12:00.800 --> 00:12:03.800
every scene there is a gap.

00:12:03.800 --> 00:12:07.800
Sometimes line to line there’s a gap.

00:12:07.800 --> 00:12:10.690
Someone asks the question, do you want to
go out tonight?

00:12:10.690 --> 00:12:12.550
The answer is where did you put the cheese?

00:12:12.550 --> 00:12:15.180
There is a gap.

00:12:15.180 --> 00:12:16.180
Do you want to go out tonight?

00:12:16.180 --> 00:12:18.330
I am expecting an answer.

00:12:18.330 --> 00:12:19.330
I didn’t get an answer.

00:12:19.330 --> 00:12:21.230
I heard the question about where is the cheese.

00:12:21.230 --> 00:12:26.070
In fact I never get to ask that question,
so those are little gaps.

00:12:26.070 --> 00:12:27.070
Life goes like that.

00:12:27.070 --> 00:12:33.200
We keep navigating and working our way through
a world that won’t give us exactly what

00:12:33.200 --> 00:12:35.130
we want because that is just the way it works.

